From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bmatusiak <bmatusiak@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 22:05:25 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] usb_dev: wipe_usb_buffer() sends the next packet to the slot
 the controller waits on

At an unlock, OnlyKey.ino answers the messages waiting for 200 ms and then
calls wipe_usb_buffer() ("Wipe old responses"). The wipe frees every packet
the USB controller still owns - one armed in an endpoint's buffer
descriptor that the host has not taken yet - and resets tx_state with the
mapping copied from SET_CONFIGURATION. That mapping has ODD_FREE and
EVEN_FREE the other way round for this case: the controller's even/odd
pointer stays on the freed packet's slot (only a bus reset, ODDRST, moves
it), so the next packet must go into that slot.

Instead the next packet went into the other slot. The controller kept
waiting on the empty one, so the packet sat unsent until the following
packet filled the slot it waited on; then both went out, the older one
second - taken by the host as the reply to its next request.

Seen with the vendor interface after a key restart (the idle lockout) and
an unlock while a host was talking to the key: the first answer after the
unlock was not delivered (the host waited out 30 s), the next request read
that answer as its own and failed. Measured on a DEBUG 3.1.0 key from the
endpoint's buffer descriptors: the answer armed in the even slot
(BDT_OWN | DATA0) stayed owned for 30 s; the next answer went to the odd
slot, and both were taken within a millisecond.

The freed packet's slot also carries the data toggle the host expects,
since the host never received that packet.
---
 usb_dev.c | 18 ++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/usb_dev.c b/usb_dev.c
index b887b52..777e30d 100644
--- a/usb_dev.c
+++ b/usb_dev.c
@@ -293,12 +293,26 @@ void wipe_usb_buffer() {
 			tx_first[i] = NULL;
 			tx_last[i] = NULL;
 			usb_rx_byte_count_data[i] = 0;
+			/*
+			 * The next packet goes where the controller waits. A packet the
+			 * controller still owned (armed, not yet taken by the host) was
+			 * freed above, but the controller's even/odd pointer for this
+			 * endpoint stays on its slot - only a bus reset (ODDRST) moves it.
+			 * ODD_FREE means the even slot was armed: the next packet is even.
+			 * EVEN_FREE means the odd slot was armed: the next is odd. (The
+			 * mapping copied from SET_CONFIGURATION had these two the other way
+			 * round: the next packet went into the slot the controller does not
+			 * look at, and sat there until the following packet filled the
+			 * other one - then both went out, the older one second, as the reply
+			 * to the wrong request.) The slot also carries the data toggle the
+			 * host expects, since it never received the freed packet.
+			 */
 			switch (tx_state[i]) {
-			  case TX_STATE_EVEN_FREE:
+			  case TX_STATE_ODD_FREE:
 			  case TX_STATE_NONE_FREE_EVEN_FIRST:
 				tx_state[i] = TX_STATE_BOTH_FREE_EVEN_FIRST;
 				break;
-			  case TX_STATE_ODD_FREE:
+			  case TX_STATE_EVEN_FREE:
 			  case TX_STATE_NONE_FREE_ODD_FIRST:
 				tx_state[i] = TX_STATE_BOTH_FREE_ODD_FIRST;
 				break;
